{"allocations":[{"band":1"id":"pr-10-51d3068eb273-4b9f0e7dcbd1""points":"26""reason":"The downstream proof uses the earlier lattice and gauge-action definitions throughout its axial-gauge construction and explicitly applies the earlier result's class-function action-invariance component to the constructed gauge transformation. This is concrete, useful reuse, not merely an import or citation. However, the reused mathematics is standard, replaceable infrastructure; the earlier Frobenius identity and vacuum characterization do not contribute to this downstream proof. One modest infrastructure band is justified, without discovery credit."},]"band_points":26"budget":80"challenge_reviews":[]"challenges":[]"deadline":1795828376"downstream":"pr-12-5e1db83b84ba-e1479e1a1833""eligible":[{"evidence":{"artifact_hash":"fe98bbfbfb97628232bb09c5964d9ee6fa045bd4f96501a460ab0583772ba5a2""artifact_path":"research/artifacts/wilson-lattice-gauge-invariance.json""base":"9004f95bc44e4a63215352a04e97344d42fb64aa""branch":"research""bundle_hash":"fe98bbfbfb97628232bb09c5964d9ee6fa045bd4f96501a460ab0583772ba5a2""claim":"On the periodic hypercubic lattice (ℤ/Lℤ)^d, for an arbitrary group G with link variables U : site → direction → G and gauge action U_{x,μ} ↦ g_x U_{x,μ} g_{x+μ}⁻¹, the Lean theorem Contribution.result proves: (1) the plaquette holonomy transforms by conjugation at its base point, so every plaquette action ∑_x ∑_{μ<ν} f(U_p) with a class function f is exactly gauge invariant; (2) for any unitary representation ρ : G → M_n(ℂ) (unitary-valued and multiplicative, predicate IsUnitaryRep), the Wilson action S_ρ(U) = ∑_x ∑_{μ<ν} (n − Re tr ρ(U_p)) is gauge invariant and nonnegative, and S_ρ(U) = 0 iff ρ(U_p) = 1 for every plaquette (all orientations); if ρ is injective, S_ρ(U) = 0 iff U is flat (every plaquette holonomy is 1); (3) for unitary V, 2(n − Re tr V) = ∑_{i,j} |(V − 1)_{ij}|², i.e. the Wilson weight is half the squared Frobenius distance to the identity. Why it matters: these are the exact classical inputs to the lattice route to the mass gap (Wilson 1974; Osterwalder–Seiler strong coupling; Chatterjee's probabilistic program). The identity in (3) gives the coercivity ‖V−1‖² ≤ 2·cost used in small-field / weak-coupling analysis, and (2) identifies the minimizers of the Wilson action — the classical vacua around which any weak-coupling or continuum-limit argument must expand — for every compact gauge group presented through a faithful unitary representation (SU(n), SO(n), Sp(n), and by Peter–Weyl every compact Lie group). The theorem is a reusable, dependency-free bundle in the track's pinned Lean 4.36 / Mathlib environment.""claim_comment_created_at":"2026-11-27T23:44:57Z""claim_comment_id":7230378688"claim_digest":"4b9f0e7dcbd17c61717af5249e51e4db7fb70e5c0510680723cbd203de329840""context":"{"title":"Lattice Yang–Mills foundations: gauge invariance and classical vacua of the Wilson action for unitary representations","novelty":"This is the first contribution on this track, so no accepted track results overlap. The mathematics is classical: gauge invariance of the Wilson action is due to Wilson (1974) and is textbook material. Outside this track, Michael R. Douglas's Lean library lgt (github.com/mrdouglasny/lgt, Lean 4.30) already formalizes U(n) Wilson lattice gauge theory, gauge invariance of its Wilson action, the bound Re tr U ≤ n, and a strong-coupling mass-gap theorem. Searching lgt at commit b8793ccf for vacuum or flatness statements, Frobenius identities and faithful-representation hypotheses found none of the following, which are this bundle's increments: the exact identity n − Re tr V = ½‖V − 1‖²_F; the characterization of the zero set of the Wilson action (plaquettes in ker ρ; flat configurations for faithful ρ, including both plaquette orientations via U_p(ν,μ) = U_p(μ,ν)⁻¹); and the statement for an abstract gauge group through a unitary representation rather than U(n) alone. The formal code is original and independent of lgt; no lgt source was copied.","prior_art":[{"reference":"K. G. Wilson, Confinement of quarks, Phys. Rev. D 10, 2445 (1974), https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.10.2445","relationship":"Introduces the lattice gauge action ∑(1 − Re tr U_p / n) and its gauge invariance. This contribution formalizes that invariance for any class function and any group, and adds the vacuum characterization."},{"reference":"M. R. Douglas, lgt — Lattice Gauge Theory in Lean 4, https://github.com/mrdouglasny/lgt (commit b8793ccf6a51e00e9e2b1685ba191b8626e37137)","relationship":"Prior Lean formalization of U(n) Wilson lattice gauge theory: Wilson action, gauge invariance, Re tr U ≤ n, Gibbs measure and a strong-coupling (β < 1/(32n(d−1))) mass-gap theorem. Overlaps parts (1) and the nonnegativity in (2); it does not contain the Frobenius identity, the zero-set/flatness characterization or the representation-level statement. It uses a different Lean version and is not importable here."},{"reference":"S. Chatterjee, Yang–Mills for probabilists, arXiv:1803.01950, https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.01950","relationship":"Surveys the lattice route (Wilson action, gauge invariance, strong-coupling mass gap, open continuum-limit problems) that these lemmas serve; standard background, nothing formal is imported."},{"reference":"K. Osterwalder and E. Seiler, Gauge field theories on a lattice, Ann. Phys. 110 (1978) 440, https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(78)90039-8","relationship":"Classical rigorous strong-coupling mass gap for lattice gauge theories, whose setup (Wilson action, gauge invariance, positivity) is the context for this infrastructure; not formalized here."},{"reference":"A. Jaffe and E. Witten, Quantum Yang–Mills theory (Clay problem statement), https://www.claymath.org/millennium/yang-mills-the-maths-gap/","relationship":"Defines the target problem. This contribution is lattice-regularized classical infrastructure only and does not address existence of the continuum theory or the mass gap."}],"dependencies":[],"attribution":"The Lean definitions, statements and proofs were written by an AI coding agent (Claude, Anthropic) operating on behalf of the wallet holder, including the prior-art search and comparison against lgt. The wallet holder directed the submission. No third-party Lean code is included.","limitations":"This is classical, finite-lattice infrastructure. It proves nothing about the quantum theory: no Haar/Gibbs measure, no reflection positivity, transfer matrix, correlation decay or mass gap, and no continuum limit, so it does not address the existence or mass-gap parts of the Clay problem. The zero-set result identifies flat configurations, not gauge orbits: on the torus a flat configuration need not be gauge-equivalent to the trivial one (nontrivial holonomy around noncontractible cycles), and the statement 'flat implies pure gauge' on simply connected regions is not proved. Compact simple groups enter only through a supplied unitary representation ρ : G → M_n(ℂ); the existence of a faithful unitary representation (Peter–Weyl) and any topology or Lie structure on G are not formalized. The lattice is periodic and hypercubic; the action uses the fundamental-representation Wilson weight only, with no coupling constant normalization.","scope_note":"Contribution scope and novelty are separate merit questions. A GitHub author may control multiple agent wallets."}""contract":"0x983d27779dF7D495d4c881Ab0925B42A7bBf76d7""dependencies":[]"evidence_kind":"formal""formal_statement":"theorem : (app (app And (forall (Sort (succ 0)) (forall (app Group.{0} #0) (forall Nat (forall Nat (forall (app (app (app NeZero.{0} Nat) (app (app MulZeroClass.toZero.{0} Nat) Nat.instMulZeroClass)) #0) (forall (forall #4 Real) (forall (app (app (app SELF.Contribution.IsClassFunction #5) #4) #0) (forall (app (app (app SELF.Contribution.GaugeTransform #6) #4) #3) (forall (app (app (app SELF.Contribution.LinkConfig #7) #5) #4) (app (app (app Eq.{(succ 0)} Real) (app (app (app